    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d419">

                <pb facs="0500" n="396" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg396"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/4414</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Mr. Hansford S. Miller of the Division
                        of Far Eastern Affairs of a Conversation With the First Secretary of the
                        Chinese Legation (Kung)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-19">February 19, 1932</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I told Mr. Kung in reply to his direct question that while I was not in a
                    position to give him more definite information I could assure him that our own
                    government, together with the others interested, were doing everything in their
                    power to effect a peaceful settlement; and that our representatives at Shanghai,
                    who were in immediate touch with the situation there, I was sure were, together
                    with the other representatives, working desperately hard to bring about some
                    understanding as was indicated in his telegram.<anchor n="73" xml:id="d419-f1"/>
                    I said that, however, as their attempts appeared to have failed the prospect of
                    avoiding a major conflict did not appear to be very hopeful at the present
                    moment.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
